{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## AC2 discriminating experiment — the Docker-cache hypothesis is FALSIFIED\n\nTwo rival consumers were live. This experiment separates them on a prediction they\ndisagree about, using only read-only CloudWatch and local `git`.\n\n**Rival A** (ticket hypothesis 2, \"autodeploy prunes Docker caches too late\"): the bytes are\nbuildkit cache + image layers from review-bot rebuilds. Build artifacts can only be created\nby a **build**, and autodeploy only builds when `main` advances with a `BOT_PATHS`/\n`SECRETS_PATHS` change. → **Predicts: in a window with no such commit, the root disk is flat.**\n\n**Rival B**: the bytes are the review-bot's ephemeral per-review working set on the\ncontainer's writable layer. → **Predicts: the disk grows at the same rate in build-free\nwindows, as long as reviews are running.**\n\nMethod: cut the 5-min `root_disk_used_percent` series at every `origin/main` commit touching\n`BOT_PATHS`/`SECRETS_PATHS`, allow 15 min after each for autodeploy to notice + build +\nprune, and measure growth inside each maximal build-free interval ≥40 min. Intervals\ncontaining a reclaim were excluded so a drop cannot confound the rate. 1 pt = 0.30 GiB.\n\n| build-free interval (UTC) | mins | Δ% | GiB/h | LLM tokens |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 08-16 23:46 → 08-17 17:18 | 1052 | +34 | **+0.59** | 23.2M |\n| 08-17 17:33 → 08-17 20:14 | 161 | +23 | **+2.67** | 8.7M |\n| 08-17 20:29 → 08-18 04:16 | 467 | +29 | **+1.12** | 20.8M |\n| 08-18 05:13 → 08-18 07:02 | 110 | +17 | **+2.91** | 14.1M |\n| 08-18 10:00 → 08-18 11:13 | 73 | +3 | +0.83 | 5.0M |\n| 08-18 11:28 → 08-18 13:07 | 100 | +5 | +0.95 | 1.3M |\n| 08-18 18:15 → 08-18 22:03 | 227 | +31 | **+2.54** | 11.2M |\n| 08-18 22:43 → 08-18 23:51 | 68 | +9 | **+2.49** | 7.8M |\n| 08-19 03:22 → 08-19 04:26 | 64 | +1 | +0.33 | 4.6M |\n| 08-19 06:39 → 08-19 07:36 | 57 | +12 | **+3.93** | 2.2M |\n| 08-19 10:39 → 08-19 12:15 | 96 | +3 | +0.57 | 3.4M |\n| 08-19 13:44 → 08-19 14:56 | 72 | +0 | **+0.00** | 0.6M |\n\nn=12; growth GiB/h min +0.00, median **+1.12**, max **+3.93**. **10 of 12 build-free\nintervals grew faster than 0.5 GiB/h**, and the single interval that did **not** grow at all\nis the one with almost no review activity (0.6M tokens).\n\n**Result: Rival A is rejected.** Buildkit cache and image layers cannot appear without a\nbuild, yet the disk climbs at up to 3.93 GiB/h across windows in which no rebuild was\npossible. The one flat interval is flat because *reviews* stopped, not because *builds*\nstopped. Rival B's prediction holds.\n\nCorroborating (E3): all **23** reclaim events over 2026-08-15→19 are preceded by a\n`BOT_PATHS` commit (22/23 within 60 min, 16/23 within 30 min); there is no reclaim in any\nbuild-free window. Reclamation is tied to the deploy, not to the pressure gate.\n\n### Where the growing bytes must be\n\n`infra/compose/docker-compose.yml:223-240` — the `review-bot` service's only mounts are\n`./rebar-bot-signing-key` (ro), `gerrit_reviewbot` → `/var/gerrit/site/reviewbot`, and\n`gerrit_reviewbot_tickets` → `/var/gerrit/site/reviewbot-tickets`; both named volumes are\nbind-backed onto the **separate 50-GiB `/var/gerrit` EBS volume**. No `tmpfs`, no other mount.\nSo every other runtime write lands on the container's writable layer = `/var/lib/docker` on\nthe **30-GiB root**.\n\n`REBAR_GATE_TMPDIR` is set **nowhere** — not in `docker-compose.yml`'s environment block, not\nin `infra/scripts/fetch-secrets.sh` (which generates the SSM-sourced `.env`), not in\n`infra/compose/Dockerfile.reviewbot`. So `store_root()`\n(`src/rebar/_snapshot/repo_snapshot.py:113`) provably falls through to\n`tempfile.gettempdir()` → **`/tmp/rebar-gate-snapshots` inside the container**, on the root\ndisk. That is the same path the alarm's own description already names as a root-disk reclaim\ntarget (`infra/terraform/monitoring_autodeploy.tf:213`).\n\nThose bytes belong to a **running** container's read-write layer: they are neither buildkit\ncache (`docker builder prune`) nor a dangling image (`docker image prune`), so\n`prune_docker_caches` cannot reclaim them **at all** — which is why the reclaim gate story\n28f9 added could not have prevented this breach no matter when it fired."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787172784687057001,"uuid":"45dda50b-46b0-425e-ab64-72fdf7249cd2"}